                "annotations": [],
                "text": "Here's the analysis of the transformation rule:\n\n1. **Identify the diagonal line**: Each input grid contains a diagonal line of non-zero pixels of a single color.\n2. **Determine the fill direction**:\n   - In Example 1: The diagonal goes from top-left `(2, 0)` down-right `(10, 8)`. The fill is to the left (from the line to column 0). Notice that rows above the line remain empty, but each row containing the line fills leftward from the pixel to the left border (column 0).\n   - In Example 2: The diagonal goes from `(0, 8)` down-left to `(8, 0)`. The fill is to the right (from the line to the right border). Rows below the line continue filling from column 0 all the way to the right border.\n   - In Example 3: The diagonal goes from `(0, 2)` down-left to `(2, 0)`. The fill is to the right.\n\nWait, why does Example 2 fill rows 9 and 10 completely, but Example 1 does not fill rows 0 and 1?\nNotice:\n- Example 1: The line starts at `(2, 0)`. For `r >= 2`, column `c = r - 2`. Everything to the left of the line `c <= r - 2` is filled with 4. If extended upwards, for `r < 2`, `c = r - 2 < 0`, so no cells have `0 <= c <= r - 2`.\n- Example 2: The line satisfies `r + c = 8`. In output, for any `(r, c)`, if `r + c >= 8`, it is filled with 3! Since `r + c >= 8`, when `r = 9`, `c >= -1`, so all columns `0..11` satisfy `9 + c >= 8`. That explains why rows 9 and 10 are completely filled!\n- Example 3: The line satisfies `r + c = 2`. The output has `r + c >= 2` filled with 2!\n\nSo it's a half-plane fill!\n- Example 1: `r - c >= 2` (i.e. `c <= r - 2`). The region `r - c >= 2` is filled with 4.\n- Example 2: `r + c >= 8`. The region `r + c >= 8` is filled with 3.\n- Example 3: `r + c >= 2`. The region `r + c >= 2` is filled with 2.\n\nIn all cases, the region filled is the \"lower\" / \"bottom\" side of the line:\n- In Ex 1, the line separates top-right from bottom-left; the bottom-left half-plane is filled.\n- In Ex 2 & 3, the line separates top-left from bottom-right; the bottom-right half-plane is filled.\nIn other words, it's always the half-plane containing larger row indices / below the line (downwards).\n\nPlease provide the test input grid.",
